    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Children vary in the extent to which they are sensitive to their environments, which then goes on to shape their developmental outcomes. Sensitive children are shown to have both more negative outcomes under conditions of adversity and more positive outcomes under conditions of support when compared with their less sensitive peers, who tend to have similar outcomes regardless of context. Not much is known, however, about sensitivity in contexts of social learning – for example, how likely social partners are to respond to requests for help from a child. In the current exploratory study, we exposed children to two doses of reliable or unreliable help from a novel social partner, measured their emotional reactivity in response to the manipulation, and then presented them with a learning task in which they had the option to seek help or explore alone. In Aim 1, we looked at children’s facial expressions, finding that children varied in emotional sensitivity to the reliability of help. Generally, children exposed to reliable help showed more positive emotion and children exposed to unreliable help showed more negative emotion; however, we also found substantial variation within each help condition. In Aim 2, we tested the relationship between children’s sensitivity – measured through variation in emotional reactivity – to reliable or unreliable help with their amount of time it took for them to first ask for help in a subsequent learning task. We found that children in the unreliable help condition who expressed more negative emotion during the second dose of unreliable help delayed their first bid for help; while, regardless of help condition, children who expressed more positive emotion during the second dose of reliable or unreliable help were more likely to ask for help sooner. This suggests that positive emotion may indicate a greater willingness to engage in social learning, regardless of how responsive a social partner is, while increased negative emotion in unreliable help contexts leads to a heightened adaptive response during future social learning tasks.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Dontha, M., Shannon, K.A., Gweon, H., and Frank, M.C. (2024). \"Unfortunately, I couldn't help you in time:\" Exploring emotional reactivity to the reliability of help. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/nv900xt8037.",
